            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.139,
            0.542,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "To find the length of the side AC, we consider"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.344,
            0.17,
            0.488,
            0.212
          ],
          "content": "\\sin 3 0 ^ { \\circ }   =   { \\frac { \\mathrm { A B } } { \\mathrm { A C } } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.56,
            0.183,
            0.639,
            0.205
          ],
          "content": "( \\mathrm { W h y } ? )",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.243,
            0.139,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.388,
            0.228,
            0.489,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": "{ \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } \\; = \\; { \\frac { 5 } { \\mathrm { A C } } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.279,
            0.14,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.376,
            0.278,
            0.507,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "AC = 10    cm",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.304,
            0.901,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": "Note that alternatively we could have used Pythagoras theorem to determine the third side in the example above,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.361,
            0.14,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.355,
            0.847,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{AC} = \\sqrt{\\mathrm{AB}^{2} + \\mathrm{BC}^{2}} = \\sqrt{5^{2} + (5\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\mathrm{cm} = 10\\mathrm{cm}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.399,
            0.534,
            0.418
          ],
          "content": "**Example 7 :** In   PQR, right-angled at"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.419,
            0.532,
            0.438
          ],
          "content": "Q (see Fig. 8.20), $\\mathrm { P Q } = 3$ cm and $\\mathrm{PR} = 6\\ \\mathrm{cm}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.438,
            0.408,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": "Determine $\\angle \\mathrm { Q P R }$ and $\\angle \\mathrm { P R Q }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.464,
            0.534,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** Given $\\mathrm { P Q } = 3$ cm and $PR = 6   cm .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.509,
            0.205,
            0.528
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.351,
            0.496,
            0.478,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{PQ}{PR} \\approx \\sin R",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.585,
            0.423,
            0.871,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.685,
                0.539,
                0.785,
                0.557
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 8.20"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.569,
            0.126,
            0.584
          ],
          "content": "or"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.553,
            0.49,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "\\sin R = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.607,
            0.135,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.32,
            0.606,
            0.461,
            0.626
          ],
          "content": "\\angle PRQ = 30^{\\circ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_23.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.636,
            0.238,
            0.654
          ],
          "content": "and therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.318,
            0.634,
            0.563,
            0.655
          ],
          "content": "\\angle  QPR = 60^{\\circ}. \\quad ( Why? )",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_25.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.666,
            0.902,
            0.723
          ],
          "content": "You may note that if one of the sides and any other part (either an acute angle or any side) of a right triangle is known, the remaining sides and angles of the triangle can be determined."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.725,
            0.901,
            0.784
          ],
          "content": "**Example 8 :** If sin $( \\mathrm { A } - \\mathrm { B } ) = \\frac { 1 } { 2 } , \\cos ( \\mathrm { A } + \\mathrm { B } ) = \\frac { 1 } { 2 } , 0 ^ { \\circ } < \\mathrm { A } + \\mathrm { B } \\leq 9 0 ^ { \\circ } , \\mathrm { A } > \\mathrm { B } .$ , find A and B."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.789,
            0.752,
            0.833
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** Since, sin $( \\mathrm { A } - \\mathrm { B } ) = { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } }$ , therefore, $\\mathrm { A - B } = 3 0 ^ { \\circ } \\quad \\mathrm { ( W h y ? ) }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.868,
            0.805,
            0.898,
            0.822
          ],
          "content": "(1)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.834,
            0.7,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": "Also, since cos $( \\mathbf { A } + \\mathbf { B } ) = { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } }$ , therefore, $\\mathrm { A + B = 6 0 ^ { \\circ } \\quad ( W h y ? ) }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.868,
            0.846,
            0.898,
            0.863
          ],
          "content": "(2)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.877,
            0.588,
            0.897
          ],
          "content": "Solving (1) and (2), we get : $\\mathrm { A }   = 4 5 ^ { \\circ }$ and $\\mathbf { B } = 1 5 ^ { \\circ }$"
        }
      ]
    }
